<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Late Bronze Age collapse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Bronze Age Collapse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> collapse was a period of widespread destruction and political breakdown in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> around 1200 BCE. Its causes are debated and probably combined drought, famine, migrations including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, internal unrest and disrupted trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> disintegrated in this period, and the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> survived but entered a long decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse.jpg" alt="Late Bronze Age collapse" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexikoua · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_Age_End.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, from about 1550 to 1200 BCE, was marked by advanced civilisations with extensive trade networks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, Mesopotamians and Mycenaeans built monumental architecture, worked metals and kept written records, and a flourishing trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, pottery and farm goods, together with diplomatic ties, deepened their interdependence. Most of these powers relied on a palace economy, in which wealth was concentrated in a central bureaucracy and then redistributed according to the ruler's wishes. This web of dependencies, together with the rigidity of the palace system, exposed the societies to the cascading effects of distant disturbances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The German historian Arnold Hermann Ludwig Heeren first dated the collapse to about 1200 BCE. The Mycenaean palaces did not survive, and the palace economy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> gave way to small village cultures of the Greek Dark Ages. In Anatolia the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> was already fragmenting under famine, plague and civil war, and its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> was burned at an unknown date in this period, though it may in fact have been abandoned. Ugarit was destroyed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> faced attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>. Ramesses III defeated them, but the expense strained the Egyptian treasury, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> shrank considerably by the mid-twelfth century BCE.</p><p>Earlier historians believed that almost every city between Pylos and Gaza was violently destroyed. More recent research by Jesse Millek found that, of 148 sites said to have been destroyed around 1200 BCE, 94 were misdated, assumed on little evidence or never destroyed at all, and some scholars now see the collapse as more limited in scale than once thought. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> survived in weakened forms, and the Phoenician city-states gained autonomy as Egyptian and Assyrian power in West Asia declined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many causes have been proposed, among them drought, volcanic eruptions, earthquakes, disease, invasions by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> and changes in warfare that ended the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>. None has won agreement, and the causes were probably several. Joseph Tainter's systems theory holds that rising complexity left societies unable to rebuild once they were seriously disrupted. Tree rings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> show a severe dry period from about 1198 to 1196 BCE.</p><p>Robert Drews called the collapse arguably the worst disaster in ancient history. Later Greek tradition remembered a lost golden age, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> set the Age of Heroes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the cruel Age of Iron. Slow changes in metallurgy then led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> of the first millennium BCE across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Late Bronze Age collapse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Bronze Age Collapse</b> from approximately 1200 BCE severed the trade networks that sustained imperial revenues and removed the Hittite rival around whom Egyptian foreign policy had been organised.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hittite Empire collapsed in the early twelfth century BCE as part of the broader <b>Bronze Age Collapse</b>, which destroyed or crippled most of the major states of the eastern Mediterranean within a generation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The collapse was part of the wider <b>Late Bronze Age collapse</b> that also brought down the Hittite Empire.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its historical backdrop is the time of the <b>Late Bronze Age collapse</b> in the early 12th century BCE, so Homer is separated from his subject by about 400 years, the period known as the Greek Dark Ages.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Late Bronze Age</b> offers a collapse on a larger scale.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Late Bronze Age collapse</b>, Til Barsip at Tell Ahmar was the Luwian city of Masuwari.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-adini">Bit Adini</a></li></ul></section>
